# Xenogears Decomp Project — Working Rules

## Identity
This is a decomp-accurate PSX-to-PC port. Retail behavior is the spec.
You have shell, git, build, and runtime access. Use them. You are
expected to form hypotheses, test them, and iterate autonomously.

## What changed from the old rules
You no longer need to stop and ask before implementing. If you have a
hypothesis and you can test it by building and running, do that. Chase
the bug. The old stop-and-report-every-step cadence produced 20 clean
audits while the real bug (rsin/rcos swap) sat one layer below the
whole chain. Autonomy found it in 25 minutes. Work that way.

## Non-negotiable guardrails (these stay)

### 1. One logical change per commit
Not for tidiness — for BISECTABILITY. When something breaks three
weeks from now you need to find it. Commit the camera fix, the RNG
change, and the build-config change SEPARATELY, even if you made them
in the same session. If you can't cleanly separate a diff, that's a
signal you conflated two things.

### 2. Never lose the make check baseline
Before starting work: record whether `make check` passes and what
fails. After: record it again. If you changed five things and the
checksum broke, you CANNOT tell what did it — the baseline is gone,
and you can no longer validate anything downstream either.
If make check state is unknown, establishing it is the FIRST task.

### 3. Build-config changes get their own scrutiny
config/*.yaml, linker scripts, symbol maps, rodata ownership, section
layout: these are not source fixes. A wrong one silently poisons match
verification for the ENTIRE tree — producing false matches and false
mismatches in unrelated files. Never bundle one with a source change.
Validate it in isolation. State explicitly what it does and why.

### 4. Diagnostics stay labeled
Any temporary print, probe, guard, or auto-confirm hack: mark it in
code AND in the commit message, with removal instructions. A
diagnostic must never quietly become permanent behavior. (5f1e799's
auto-confirm is TEST TOOLING, not a fix — keep it labeled as such.)

### 5. Don't invent game logic
If retail behavior is unknown, say so. Do not fill a gap with a
plausible guess, a "reasonable" default, or a silent fallback. Cite
asm, matched C, or a runtime capture. "Seems right" is not evidence.

### 6. Say what you actually proved
- Built but not run → "build-verified only"
- Ran but didn't compare to retail → say that
- Never claim "fixed" or "verified" without runtime evidence
Negative results are valuable. Disproving your own hypothesis with
evidence is a good outcome, not a failure.

## Known trap: negative greps are unreliable here
The port stores flags as BITFIELDS and named struct members where
retail uses literal masks. Grepping C for `0x800000` will miss
`scriptFlags_0x16`. This has already produced one wrong "no writer
exists" conclusion. Search the ASM and the struct definitions, not
just C literals.

## Known trap: stale handoff notes
Before targeting a "known next blocker" from an older note, verify it
hasn't already been landed. `git log --oneline -S'<symbol>'` or just
read the handler. This cost a full cycle on opcode 0x86, which was
already implemented.

## Environment
- `XENO_KERNEL_SEL=0` — required, avoids false-positive spin-wait
- `XENO_FIELD_TEST=1` — gates field diagnostics (incl. auto-confirm)
- Build: `./pc_port/build_port.sh` (expect LINK OK)
- Matching: `make check` (needs disc data in disc/)
- Log meaningful cycles to `scratchpad/grind_log.md`

## Working style
Bounded scope is still preferred — chase one blocker at a time, and
don't refactor unrelated code during a bug hunt. But "bounded" means
"don't wander," not "stop and ask." If a fix requires touching an
adjacent function, do it and say so. If you discover a second bug
while fixing the first, note it and either fix it in a SEPARATE commit
or add it to the queue.

Prefer the smallest change that's actually correct. A hack that masks
the real bug is worse than no fix.
